# Citrus Writing — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in what you need. Every value below is OPTIONAL: with none of them
# set the app runs completely, on a local SQLite file, with password sign-in and a development
# plan switch standing in for checkout. What each block buys you is written above it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database ---
# A Postgres connection string. Required — there is no local fallback (src/lib/db/database-url.ts
# throws if it is unset). Used identically by the app, the Prisma CLI (prisma7.config.ts, which
# does not read .env itself — both import this same file), and every npm run db:* script.
# Supabase's connection pooler (transaction mode, port 6543) is the right choice for the app's
# short-lived request connections; a direct connection (port 5432) also works for scripts.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres"

# ------------------------------------------------------------ sign-in with ---
# "Continue with Google" / "Continue with GitHub" on the sign-in and sign-up cards.
#
# A provider whose two variables are not both set is not configured, and the app treats that as
# "does not exist": its button is never rendered and its route returns 404. So leaving this
# block empty is a supported state, not a broken one.
#
# Redirect URI to register with each provider:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/oauth/google/callback
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/oauth/github/callback
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- payments ---
# Stripe Checkout for the Serial plan, plus the Billing Portal for cards, invoices and
# cancelling. Called over the REST API with `fetch` — there is no `stripe` package to install.
#
# WITHOUT STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, upgrading switches the plan directly and says so, so every plan
# gate can be exercised end to end before a Stripe account exists. That fallback is refused in
# production, so a deploy missing its keys cannot quietly give the paid plan away.
#
# The webhook is what grants the plan — never the browser returning from Checkout. Point it at
#   /api/stripe/webhook
# and subscribe to: checkout.session.completed, customer.subscription.created,
# customer.subscription.updated, customer.subscription.deleted.
# Locally: stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
# The payment form is EMBEDDED (ui_mode "form"), so the browser needs the publishable key too.
# Next only exposes variables prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ to the client, and that is correct here —
# the publishable key is meant to be public. The SECRET key must never carry that prefix.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STRIPE_PRICE_SERIAL_MONTHLY=
# STRIPE_PRICE_SERIAL_YEARLY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- email ---
# The one email this app sends: the password-reset link.
#
# Without a key the message is printed to the server log instead — the "forgotten password"
# screen says so explicitly rather than leaving someone waiting for mail that is not coming.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# MAIL_FROM="Citrus Writing <hello@your-domain.example>"

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- seed ---
# The demo account `npm run db:seed` creates. Defaults shown; override to seed your own.
# SEED_EMAIL=demo@citruswriting.app
# SEED_PASSWORD=lantern-tide-demo
# SEED_PEN_NAME="The Lamplighter"
